Molluscum Contagiosum
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Pearly dome-shaped papules, Central umbilication, Clustered distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-030
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of pain, swelling, and redness surrounding a cluster of chronic skin bumps, causing severe parental anxiety regarding a spreading skin infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a pediatric patient with an acute, painful, erythematous flare surrounding a cluster of pre-existing skin lesions in the groin. Urgent assessment was required to differentiate a severe secondary staphylococcal cellulitis or abscess from the benign "BOTE" (Beginning of the End) sign of Molluscum, avoiding unnecessary systemic antibiotics and alleviating acute caregiver distress before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "These little bumps have been there for months, but today the whole area turned bright red, swollen, and it's too painful for them to walk normally."
History of Present Illness: The patient developed a clustered distribution of firm dome-shaped papules in the right inguinal flexural region approximately 4 months ago. They have been generally asymptomatic. Over the last 48 hours, the parent noted the sudden development of a surrounding eczematous reaction. Today, several of the pearly or flesh-coloured lesions have become acutely inflamed, erythematous, and mildly tender. The parent describes a classic autoinoculation pattern, resulting in linear configurations along scratch marks. The sudden redness and localized pain prompted fears of a severe spreading bacterial infection. The child exhibits mild discomfort with ambulation due to friction.
Functional Status: Independent, currently walking with a slight limp to avoid friction.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver anxiety regarding a severe soft tissue infection.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 102/64 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 25 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable when sitting, mild distress upon direct palpation of the groin.
Physical Exam: The right inguinal crease and upper inner thigh demonstrate a cluster of approximately 15 firm, 2-4 mm dome-shaped papules. Close inspection reveals characteristic central umbilication on the uninflamed lesions. However, several central papules are currently surrounded by a striking, 3x4 cm patch of bright erythema and mild oedema (the "BOTE" sign). There is no deep induration, true fluctuant abscess formation, or ascending lymphangitic streaking. A mild, dry eczematous reaction encircles the entire cluster. No purulent discharge is actively weeping. The patient is completely afebrile.
Environmental Observations: Normal home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Inflamed Molluscum Contagiosum (BOTE Sign - Beginning of the End) - Status: Acute inflammatory phase
Differentials:

Bacterial Cellulitis / Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of classic umbilicated pearly papules at the center of the erythema, the lack of thick honey-colored crusting or true purulence, and the absence of high fever or spreading induration.

Genital Warts (Condyloma Acuminata) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct dome-shaped, smooth, umbilicated appearance, rather than the hyperkeratotic, cauliflower-like appearance of HPV warts.

Varicella (Chickenpox) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic timeline (4 months), localized clustered flexural distribution, and lack of a widespread, actively blistering rash with systemic fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute inflammatory flare of pre-existing Molluscum Contagiosum, a highly characteristic presentation known as the BOTE (Beginning of the End) sign, which signals an impending immune response and viral clearance. Triage accurately flagged the rapid onset of erythema and pain in a pediatric groin as requiring urgent evaluation to rule out deep cellulitis. Immediate after-hours assessment was crucial to correctly identify the benign viral etiology, explicitly rule out a bacterial superinfection requiring systemic antibiotics, and provide immense clinical reassurance to the panicked parents. Diagnosing this inflammatory phase accurately prevents the inappropriate use of antibiotics and averts an unnecessary emergency department visit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassured the parents that the acute redness is a positive sign of the immune system finally recognizing and attacking the virus. Prescribed a mild topical corticosteroid (Hydrocortisone 1% cream) to be applied strictly to the surrounding red eczematous skin twice daily to reduce itch and discomfort. Advised simple analgesia (Paracetamol) if painful.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying a thick barrier cream (like zinc oxide) over the bumps before dressing, you will physically stop the friction from their clothing, instantly reducing the pain when walking. By keeping your child's fingernails clipped extremely short and washing their hands frequently, you will prevent them from scratching the inflamed bumps and spreading the virus to other parts of their body.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-4 weeks to monitor the expected resolution of the lesions.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the red area becomes hard, hot, and rapidly spreads down the leg, if a large, painful pocket of thick yellow pus forms, or if the child develops a fever over 38.0C, present to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While the redness is typically a sign of healing, broken skin always carries a small risk of developing a true secondary bacterial infection.
Clinical Course: This red, inflamed phase usually lasts 1 to 2 weeks. Following this, the bumps will likely crust over, shrink, and completely disappear over the next few months.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the red area daily. The redness should stay localized strictly around the bumps. Seek medical review if it spreads rapidly or becomes deeply hot and hard.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus has hidden from your child's immune system for months. The sudden redness and swelling means the immune cells have finally found the virus and are actively destroying it. We only treat the itch; we want the immune system to finish the job.
Actionable Steps: Do not pop or squeeze the bumps, as this will spread the highly contagious core to nearby skin. Apply the mild steroid cream only to the itchy, red, flat skin around the bumps.

